  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nCurrent-Thu...High pressure over the Great Lakes extending into the \nDeep South will allow for a continuation of NERLY winds. A rather \ntight pressure gradient continues across the area supporting 10-15 \nmph winds with gusts to 20-25 mph during the day. Speeds drop to 5-\n10 mph at night and perhaps a little higher at the coast. Enough \nmoisture will exist for occasional (20-30%) onshore-moving showers \nalong the coast, esp Cape southward today; mostly St. Lucie/Martin \ncounties on Thu. Marine stratocu will push onshore with times of \nbroken CIGs, esp near the coast. Near seasonable max temps each \nday with L80s for most today, though a bit cooler on Thu \nU70s-L80s. Min temps mostly in the 60s with U50s across the \ninterior. \n\nFri-Tue...The weather pattern again becomes unsettled with energy \naloft and surface low pressure developing along an old frontal\nboundary near or northeast of the Bahamas. Moisture deepens and \nrain chances increase Fri night into the weekend. The pressure \ngradient tightens, once again, and NE/ENE winds strengthen. \nExpect winds Fri afternoon 15-20 mph and frequently gusty. Very \nwindy conditions expected Sat/Sun with speeds increasing 20-30 mph\nsustained with higher gusts. Wind Advisories may be necessary, at\nleast for the coast. Expect windy/gusty conditions to continue \ninto early next week as the pgrad is slow to relax. \n\nThese strong winds will coincide with high astronomical tides and \nhelp produce another hazardous coastal event. Beach erosion, rough \nsurf, minor coastal flooding, and dangerous rip currents beginning \nas soon as Fri. Near seasonal high temps in the U70s-L80s, but above \nnormal overnight lows in the M60s across the interior and U60s-L70s \nat the coast due to the strong onshore flow.",
